WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.232
Network input should not become trusted just because it arrives on an expected port or appears to come from an expected component.

2
00:00:08.232 --> 00:00:12.348
A secure design treats the protocol boundary as a formal contract.

3
00:00:12.348 --> 00:00:20.581
The contract should define how messages are framed, which commands are allowed, which field types and encodings are valid, how versions are

4
00:00:20.581 --> 00:00:24.323
negotiated, what authentication is expected, and how errors are reported.

5
00:00:24.323 --> 00:00:28.065
TCP is a byte stream, not an application message API.

6
00:00:28.065 --> 00:00:34.426
One receive operation may return part of a message, multiple messages, or no complete message at all.

7
00:00:34.426 --> 00:00:41.910
The application or protocol library must know how to identify a complete frame before it parses fields or makes decisions.

8
00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:49.394
Datagram-oriented designs such as UDP have different assumptions: each datagram has boundaries, but delivery, ordering, and size behavior require

9
00:00:49.394 --> 00:00:50.516
separate design choices.

10
00:00:50.516 --> 00:00:53.510
Ambiguous parsing is a security and reliability risk.

11
00:00:53.510 --> 00:01:00.994
If two components interpret the same bytes differently, the system can make inconsistent decisions about identity, authorization, size, or state.

12
00:01:00.994 --> 00:01:07.729
The safest protocols define length limits, allowed encodings, ordering rules, version behavior, and rejection behavior in one place.

13
00:01:07.729 --> 00:01:12.968
That definition should be testable, not just implied by whichever parser was written first.

14
00:01:12.968 --> 00:01:18.206
Invalid, incomplete, oversized, unexpected, or out-of-order data should fail safely and consistently.

15
00:01:18.206 --> 00:01:25.316
A defensive protocol parser releases temporary resources, avoids partial state commits, and returns clear errors without leaking unnecessary internal

16
00:01:25.316 --> 00:01:25.690
detail.

17
00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:32.800
The goal is predictable behavior under imperfect network conditions, because imperfect conditions are the normal case for socket programs.
